e connecting a severity/level designation (MSI-H) and a mechanistic explanation (dMMR) and also ties to a clinical scenario (testing in recurrence). This indicates that the community is structured around biomarker definition, interpretation, and clinical testing context rather than around specific institutions or individuals. [Data: Entities (2158, 3837, 3836); Relationships (7946, 3709)]\"},{\"summary\":\"MSI-H is explicitly defined as the high form of MSI and reflects extensive microsatellite changes\",\"explanation\":\"MSI-H (Microsatellite Instability-High) is described as a high level of microsatellite instability in cancer cells, reflecting an abnormally large number of microsatellite changes. The dataset also states directly that MSI-H is the high form of microsatellite instability, establishing a clear subtype/level relationship between MSI and MSI-H. [Data: Entities (3837, 2158); Relationships (7946)]\\n\\nThis explicit linkage supports interpreting MSI-H as a specific categorization within the broader MSI concept, rather than a separate biomarker. In practical terms (as represented in the data), MSI-H is used to convey degree of instability, which can then be contextualized by mismatch repair status. [Data: Entities (2158, 3837); Relationships (7946)]\"},{\"summary\":\"dMMR is positioned as a causal/mechanistic driver of MSI-H and is linked to dMMR genes\",\"explanation\":\"Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) is described as a condition in which mismatch repair function is impaired, allowing microsatellite errors to persist; it is described as a cause of MSI-H in cancers. This places dMMR as a mechanistic explanation for why MSI-H occurs. [Data: Entities (3836, 3837)]\\n\\nA relationship further notes that “MSI-H is stated as often due to dMMR genes,” connecting dMMR to a gene-level framing (DMMR GENES). While the dataset does not enumerate specific genes, it does establish that gene-level deficiencies leading to mismatch repair dysfunction are a commonly cited basis for MSI-H. [Data: Relationships (7947); Entities (3836)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Testing context: MSI testing is noted as generally recommended in recurrent ovarian cancer\",\"explanation\":\"A key contextual relationship states that testing for MSI is generally recommended for ovarian cancer that returns after treatment (recurrence). This ties the biomarker concept (MSI) to a specific clinical decision point—recurrent disease—suggesting that MSI assessment can become more relevant when cancer behavior changes or when additional decision support is needed after initial therapy. [Data: Relationships (3709); Entities (2158)]\\n\\nThe data does not specify how testing changes management, nor does it list guidelines, thresholds, or test modalities; it only captures the stated recommendation context for recurrence in ovarian cancer. Any further inference beyond this recurrence-linked recommendation is not supported within the provided records. [Data: Relationships (3709)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community emphasizes joint interpretation of MSI and MMR status in characterizing DNA repair abnormalities\",\"explanation\":\"MSI is described as typically assessed on tumor tissue and often evaluated alongside or in conjunction with MMR status to characterize underlying DNA repair abnormalities. This indicates that MSI is not treated in isolation in the provided description; rather, it is part of a biomarker pair (MSI and MMR status) used to interpret DNA repair function in tumors. [Data: Entities (2158)]\\n\\nBecause MSI-H is commonly associated with dMMR (and dMMR is described as causal of MSI-H), the community structure supports a layered interpretation: MSI level (including MSI-H) provides a phenotypic readout, while MMR/dMMR provides a mechanistic explanation. This is a conceptual workflow captured by the entity descriptions and relationships. [Data: Entities (2158, 3836, 3837); Relationships (7946)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Cancer-type specificity is mentioned: MSI is described